1. A method of fail-safe shutoff in an implantable drug delivery pump, the method comprising:

   delivering a therapeutic fluid from a reservoir of the implantable drug delivery pump through a flow path toward an outlet;

   monitoring, by control circuitry of the implantable drug delivery pump, at least one operating condition associated with delivery of the therapeutic fluid;

   detecting, by the control circuitry, a shutoff condition corresponding to an abnormal or predetermined fail-safe state;

   in response to detecting the shutoff condition, energizing an induction coil positioned in inductive coupling proximity to a ferromagnetic mesh disposed adjacent to a hydrophobic plug located in or adjacent to the flow path;

   inductively heating the ferromagnetic mesh by an alternating electromagnetic field generated by the induction coil;

   transferring heat from the ferromagnetic mesh to the hydrophobic plug to melt or soften at least a portion of the hydrophobic plug; and

   causing the melted or softened hydrophobic plug to occlude the flow path, thereby stopping flow of the therapeutic fluid from the reservoir toward the outlet.
